# Emerging Threats Open -- curated for what this appliance can inspect.
#
# Every rule here matches on an application-layer buffer: an HTTP header, a
# TLS SNI or certificate field, a DNS name, a JA3 hash, a file name. Those
# buffers are produced by a parser, only for the flows that have them, and are
# small.
#
# Rules that match on the raw payload are not here, and the reason is
# measured. On this unit, replaying its own traffic at 550 packets per second:
#
#     8,698 application-layer rules    34.6% CPU     0.03% loss
#     1,202 raw-payload rules          88.9% CPU    28.49% loss
#
# Adding just 100 raw-payload rules to the set above takes loss from 0.03% to
# 17.73%. It is a step, not a slope: the first of them switches on full
# reassembled-stream inspection for every TCP flow, and after that the count
# barely matters.
#
# The trade is not 1,202 detections against nothing. It is 1,202 detections
# against every other rule seeing a third less traffic -- because at 36% loss
# the whole ruleset misses a third of the packets, raw and application-layer
# alike. Fewer rules, inspecting everything, catch more than more rules
# inspecting two thirds.
#
# Raw-payload rules belong on hardware that can afford them. Measure with
# CAPTURE=<your traffic> tests/tools/rate_cpu_bench.sh before adding any.
#
